The single fact that resolves most `this` confusion: **the same function can have a different `this` on every call**, because `this` is determined by *how* the function is called, not where its code lives.

```javascript
function whoAmI() { return this.name; }

const alice = { name: "Alice", whoAmI };
const bob   = { name: "Bob",   whoAmI };

alice.whoAmI(); // "Alice"
bob.whoAmI();   // "Bob"
```

One function, `whoAmI`, defined once — but `this` inside it is a different object on each call, because it's the object *to the left of the dot* at the call site that decides it, not anything about the function's own definition.

Passing a method as a bare reference — as an event handler, a callback, a destructured value — strips it from the object it came from, and the implicit-binding rule that depended on `obj.method()` no longer applies:

```javascript
class Timer {
  constructor() { this.seconds = 0; }
  tick() { this.seconds += 1; console.log(this.seconds); }
}
const t = new Timer();
setTimeout(t.tick, 1000); // TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined
```

`setTimeout(t.tick, ...)` passes the **function itself**, detached from `t`. When the timer fires, it calls `tick()` bare — rule 5 applies, `this` is `undefined` (strict mode), and `this.seconds += 1` throws. The method losing its receiver, not `setTimeout` itself, is the bug — the exact same detachment happens with `<button onclick={obj.handler}>`, array callbacks, and destructuring a method off an object.

Two standard fixes for the `Timer` bug, and they solve it by different means:

```javascript
class Timer {
  constructor() {
    this.seconds = 0;
    this.tick = this.tick.bind(this); // fix 1: bind in the constructor
  }
  tick() { this.seconds += 1; }
}

class TimerArrow {
  seconds = 0;
  tick = () => { this.seconds += 1; }; // fix 2: class field arrow, lexical this
}
```

`bind` creates a **new function** permanently locked to `this`, assigned back onto the instance — every instance gets its own bound copy. The class-field arrow works because arrow functions never have their own `this`; being defined as a class field means "the enclosing scope" is the constructor, so `this` is lexically the instance, forever, with no bind step needed.

Both cost one function object per instance instead of one shared on the prototype — that's the trade-off for methods that get passed around as callbacks.

A plain (non-arrow) function declared **inside** a method does not inherit the method's `this` — it gets its own, decided by how *it* is called, which is almost always bare:

```javascript
const obj = {
  name: "Widget",
  process(items) {
    items.forEach(function (item) {
      console.log(this.name, item); // this.name is undefined — wrong this!
    });
  },
};
```

`forEach` calls its callback bare (`callback(item)`), so rule 5 applies inside it regardless of what `this` was in `process`. This is exactly why arrow functions became the default for inline callbacks — `items.forEach((item) => console.log(this.name, item))` inherits `process`'s `this` lexically, with no `bind` or `that = this` workaround needed.

A reliable interview method: for every function call in the snippet, ask **"what's immediately to the left of the parentheses?"** and apply the decision tree from earlier — never assume a nested function inherits anything unless it's an arrow.

```javascript
const app = {
  name: "App",
  init() {
    console.log(this.name);            // obj.method() → "App"
    setTimeout(function () {
      console.log(this.name);          // bare call inside setTimeout → undefined
    }, 0);
    setTimeout(() => {
      console.log(this.name);          // arrow → lexical, still "App"
    }, 0);
  },
};
app.init();
```

Three calls, three different answers, from one method. Reading `this` correctly is a mechanical trace, not a guess — that's exactly the skill a "predict the output" question is testing.
